= Game theory =

== Overview ==
'''Game theory''' is the formal study of strategic interaction in which each participant's outcome can depend on the choices of multiple participants.

A game specifies players, available strategies or actions, information, and payoffs or preferences over outcomes. Strategic reasoning differs from single-agent decision theory because each participant must account for others' possible behavior.

Game theory includes cooperative and non-cooperative models, simultaneous and sequential games, complete and incomplete information, repeated interaction, bargaining, evolutionary games, and many other structures.

A Nash equilibrium is a strategy profile in which no player can improve its payoff by unilaterally deviating, given the strategies of the others. Equilibrium does not automatically imply fairness, efficiency, uniqueness, or empirical realism.

== Boundaries ==
* game theory ≠ zero-sum games only
* equilibrium ≠ social optimum
* game-theoretic model ≠ prediction without behavioral/institutional assumptions

